1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,664
Your code is ready.

2
00:00:01,664 --> 00:00:03,503
Your build commands are ready.

3
00:00:03,503 --> 00:00:04,422
But every time,

4
00:00:04,422 --> 00:00:06,444
someone clicks around in Jenkins,

5
00:00:06,444 --> 00:00:08,344
runs things in the right order,

6
00:00:08,344 --> 00:00:10,121
and hopes nothing was missed.

7
00:00:10,671 --> 00:00:15,749
What if the whole delivery process was written down, in one file, right next to your code?

8
00:00:16,299 --> 00:00:18,250
That file is the Jenkinsfile.

9
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:20,396
It is how real teams run Jenkins.

10
00:00:20,396 --> 00:00:22,868
The pipeline lives in version control,

11
00:00:22,868 --> 00:00:24,234
so you can review it,

12
00:00:24,234 --> 00:00:24,884
change it,

13
00:00:24,884 --> 00:00:26,965
and go back to an older version.

14
00:00:27,515 --> 00:00:29,001
Today you learn its parts,

15
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:30,315
and you build one live,

16
00:00:30,315 --> 00:00:33,915
from a single stage to a pipeline that tells you when it fails.

17
00:00:35,465 --> 00:00:36,894
Here is what to expect.

18
00:00:37,444 --> 00:00:41,796
One: what a pipeline is, and the five parts every Jenkinsfile has.

19
00:00:42,346 --> 00:00:46,144
Two: declarative versus scripted, and why we use declarative.

20
00:00:46,694 --> 00:00:51,942
Three: a live build. We write a first pipeline, then grow it to build, test and deploy.

21
00:00:52,492 --> 00:00:56,588
Four: the post section, which runs when a build ends, pass or fail.

22
00:00:57,138 --> 00:01:00,018
This is objective three of the Jenkins domain.

23
00:01:02,068 --> 00:01:03,988
The anatomy of a Jenkinsfile.

24
00:01:04,538 --> 00:01:07,354
Everything sits inside one block called pipeline.

25
00:01:07,904 --> 00:01:09,870
Agent says where the work runs.

26
00:01:09,870 --> 00:01:12,534
Agent any means: on any available machine.

27
00:01:12,534 --> 00:01:15,008
Later you will choose a specific agent.

28
00:01:15,558 --> 00:01:17,806
Stages holds the list of stages.

29
00:01:17,806 --> 00:01:19,563
A stage is a named phase,

30
00:01:19,563 --> 00:01:20,335
like Build,

31
00:01:20,335 --> 00:01:21,389
Test or Deploy.

32
00:01:21,389 --> 00:01:23,427
These names appear on screen,

33
00:01:23,427 --> 00:01:24,902
so choose clear ones.

34
00:01:25,452 --> 00:01:26,638
Inside each stage,

35
00:01:26,638 --> 00:01:28,615
steps holds the real commands.

36
00:01:28,615 --> 00:01:30,395
A step can print a message,

37
00:01:30,395 --> 00:01:31,713
run a shell command,

38
00:01:31,713 --> 00:01:32,833
or call a plugin.

39
00:01:33,383 --> 00:01:34,563
Post is optional.

40
00:01:34,563 --> 00:01:36,298
It runs after the stages,

41
00:01:36,298 --> 00:01:38,033
and can react to success,

42
00:01:38,033 --> 00:01:38,796
to failure,

43
00:01:38,796 --> 00:01:39,698
or to always.

44
00:01:40,248 --> 00:01:42,922
You will also meet environment for variables,

45
00:01:42,922 --> 00:01:44,646
options such as a time limit,

46
00:01:44,646 --> 00:01:46,904
and when conditions that skip a stage.

47
00:01:47,454 --> 00:01:51,699
Five words to remember: pipeline, agent, stages, steps, post.

48
00:01:53,249 --> 00:01:54,828
Declarative, or scripted?

49
00:01:55,378 --> 00:01:57,840
Jenkins pipelines come in two styles.

50
00:01:57,840 --> 00:01:59,570
Scripted is the older one.

51
00:01:59,570 --> 00:02:01,100
It is full programming,

52
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:02,964
in a language called Groovy.

53
00:02:02,964 --> 00:02:03,962
It is powerful,

54
00:02:03,962 --> 00:02:05,426
and easy to get wrong.

55
00:02:05,976 --> 00:02:07,773
Declarative is the newer one.

56
00:02:07,773 --> 00:02:09,322
It has a fixed structure,

57
00:02:09,322 --> 00:02:10,623
the one you just saw.

58
00:02:10,623 --> 00:02:11,924
It is easier to read,

59
00:02:11,924 --> 00:02:14,402
and Jenkins can check it before it runs.

60
00:02:14,952 --> 00:02:19,432
As a beginner, and in most teams, use declarative. We use it in every lesson.

61
00:02:19,982 --> 00:02:21,601
Where does the file live?

62
00:02:21,601 --> 00:02:22,378
Two options.

63
00:02:22,378 --> 00:02:23,609
Typed into the job,

64
00:02:23,609 --> 00:02:25,357
which is fine for learning.

65
00:02:25,357 --> 00:02:25,552
Or,

66
00:02:25,552 --> 00:02:26,393
in real life,

67
00:02:26,393 --> 00:02:30,214
in a file named Jenkinsfile at the top of your repository,

68
00:02:30,214 --> 00:02:32,351
so it is versioned with the code.

69
00:02:32,351 --> 00:02:36,366
In the next lessons we move it into the CloudCart repository.

70
00:02:37,916 --> 00:02:39,277
Now the real thing.

71
00:02:39,277 --> 00:02:40,351
A real Jenkins,

72
00:02:40,351 --> 00:02:42,142
one continuous recording.

73
00:02:42,142 --> 00:02:43,502
I sign in as admin,

74
00:02:43,502 --> 00:02:46,582
and I type the Jenkinsfile inside the job,

75
00:02:46,582 --> 00:02:48,945
so you can see every line appear.

76
00:02:49,495 --> 00:02:53,719
From the dashboard, I click New Item. This is how every new job starts.

77
00:02:55,269 --> 00:02:58,030
I give the job a name: cloudcart-pipeline.

78
00:02:58,030 --> 00:02:59,506
Then I choose Pipeline,

79
00:02:59,506 --> 00:03:01,304
the type for a Jenkinsfile,

80
00:03:01,304 --> 00:03:02,203
and click OK.

81
00:03:02,753 --> 00:03:04,624
This is the configure page.

82
00:03:04,624 --> 00:03:06,079
On the left: General,

83
00:03:06,079 --> 00:03:06,702
Triggers,

84
00:03:06,702 --> 00:03:08,227
Pipeline and Advanced.

85
00:03:08,227 --> 00:03:09,405
I click Pipeline.

86
00:03:09,405 --> 00:03:12,176
Here is the heart of it: the Definition.

87
00:03:12,176 --> 00:03:14,671
Pipeline script means: type it here.

88
00:03:14,671 --> 00:03:18,828
Pipeline script from SCM means: read it from a repository.

89
00:03:18,828 --> 00:03:19,521
Right now,

90
00:03:19,521 --> 00:03:20,075
we type.

91
00:03:20,625 --> 00:03:21,414
Version one.

92
00:03:21,414 --> 00:03:23,452
The smallest pipeline possible.

93
00:03:23,452 --> 00:03:24,043
Pipeline,

94
00:03:24,043 --> 00:03:24,700
agent any,

95
00:03:24,700 --> 00:03:26,212
one stage called Hello,

96
00:03:26,212 --> 00:03:28,512
and one step that prints a message.

97
00:03:28,512 --> 00:03:29,564
Watch it appear.

98
00:03:30,114 --> 00:03:30,944
I click Save.

99
00:03:30,944 --> 00:03:33,052
Jenkins takes me to the job page.

100
00:03:33,052 --> 00:03:33,818
On the left,

101
00:03:33,818 --> 00:03:35,031
there is Build Now.

102
00:03:35,031 --> 00:03:35,734
I click it.

103
00:03:35,734 --> 00:03:37,202
Jenkins queues a build,

104
00:03:37,202 --> 00:03:39,757
and a build number appears under Builds.

105
00:03:40,307 --> 00:03:41,402
I open the build,

106
00:03:41,402 --> 00:03:42,691
then Console Output.

107
00:03:42,691 --> 00:03:45,527
Read it from the top: started by user admin,

108
00:03:45,527 --> 00:03:46,880
the pipeline running,

109
00:03:46,880 --> 00:03:47,911
the stage Hello,

110
00:03:47,911 --> 00:03:48,685
our message,

111
00:03:48,685 --> 00:03:49,651
and at the end,

112
00:03:49,651 --> 00:03:50,811
Finished: SUCCESS.

113
00:03:50,811 --> 00:03:52,616
That is your first pipeline.

114
00:03:56,166 --> 00:03:58,581
Now a pipeline closer to real life.

115
00:03:58,581 --> 00:03:59,823
I click Configure,

116
00:03:59,823 --> 00:04:01,687
go to the Pipeline section,

117
00:04:01,687 --> 00:04:03,274
and replace the script.

118
00:04:03,274 --> 00:04:04,654
Three stages: Build,

119
00:04:04,654 --> 00:04:05,758
Test and Deploy.

120
00:04:05,758 --> 00:04:07,345
Each runs a shell step.

121
00:04:07,345 --> 00:04:09,623
For now they only print messages.

122
00:04:09,623 --> 00:04:12,038
In the next lessons they run Maven.

123
00:04:12,588 --> 00:04:12,924
Save,

124
00:04:12,924 --> 00:04:13,931
then Build Now.

125
00:04:13,931 --> 00:04:15,073
When it finishes,

126
00:04:15,073 --> 00:04:16,215
I open the build,

127
00:04:16,215 --> 00:04:18,902
and click Pipeline Overview on the left.

128
00:04:18,902 --> 00:04:19,775
Look at this.

129
00:04:19,775 --> 00:04:20,985
One box per stage,

130
00:04:20,985 --> 00:04:21,656
all green,

131
00:04:21,656 --> 00:04:23,537
with the time each one took.

132
00:04:23,537 --> 00:04:25,687
This is why we name stages well.

133
00:04:29,237 --> 00:04:29,587
Next,

134
00:04:29,587 --> 00:04:30,778
the post section.

135
00:04:30,778 --> 00:04:32,389
I open Configure again,

136
00:04:32,389 --> 00:04:33,931
and add it at the end.

137
00:04:33,931 --> 00:04:35,542
Always runs every time.

138
00:04:35,542 --> 00:04:38,344
Success runs only when the build passes.

139
00:04:38,344 --> 00:04:40,586
Failure runs only when it fails.

140
00:04:41,136 --> 00:04:41,978
I click Save,

141
00:04:41,978 --> 00:04:43,274
and Build Now again.

142
00:04:43,274 --> 00:04:44,311
When it is done,

143
00:04:44,311 --> 00:04:45,412
I open the build,

144
00:04:45,412 --> 00:04:46,643
and Console Output.

145
00:04:46,643 --> 00:04:47,680
At the very end,

146
00:04:47,680 --> 00:04:50,336
our post messages appear: the always one,

147
00:04:50,336 --> 00:04:51,632
and the success one.

148
00:04:55,182 --> 00:04:57,413
Now the important test: a failure.

149
00:04:57,413 --> 00:04:58,922
I open Configure again,

150
00:04:58,922 --> 00:05:00,693
go to the Pipeline section,

151
00:05:00,693 --> 00:05:03,121
and add an exit 1 to the Test step,

152
00:05:03,121 --> 00:05:05,089
so that it ends with an error.

153
00:05:05,089 --> 00:05:05,418
Save,

154
00:05:05,418 --> 00:05:06,467
and build again.

155
00:05:07,017 --> 00:05:08,132
I open the build,

156
00:05:08,132 --> 00:05:09,640
then Pipeline Overview.

157
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:11,280
The Test stage turns red.

158
00:05:11,280 --> 00:05:12,394
Deploy never ran,

159
00:05:12,394 --> 00:05:15,149
because a failed stage stops the pipeline.

160
00:05:15,149 --> 00:05:16,657
Post Actions still ran,

161
00:05:16,657 --> 00:05:18,952
so the failure message was printed.

162
00:05:18,952 --> 00:05:22,558
This is the right place to send a warning to your team.

163
00:05:22,558 --> 00:05:24,788
In the console: Finished: FAILURE.

164
00:05:29,338 --> 00:05:34,991
Write, save, build, read, fix. That is the cycle you will repeat in every lesson ahead.

165
00:05:35,541 --> 00:05:36,543
From one stage,

166
00:05:36,543 --> 00:05:38,279
to a pipeline that builds,

167
00:05:38,279 --> 00:05:38,680
tests,

168
00:05:38,680 --> 00:05:40,016
deploys and reports.

169
00:05:40,016 --> 00:05:41,685
That is the Jenkinsfile.

170
00:05:43,235 --> 00:05:44,984
The post section, in detail.

171
00:05:45,534 --> 00:05:50,228
Always runs every time. Good for cleaning up, and for saving test reports.

172
00:05:50,778 --> 00:05:54,852
Success runs only when everything passed. Good for a happy message.

173
00:05:55,402 --> 00:05:57,926
Failure runs only when something failed.

174
00:05:57,926 --> 00:05:58,936
Good for alerts,

175
00:05:58,936 --> 00:06:01,397
an email or a chat message to the team.

176
00:06:01,947 --> 00:06:02,875
There are more,

177
00:06:02,875 --> 00:06:03,741
like unstable,

178
00:06:03,741 --> 00:06:05,411
for tests that fail softly,

179
00:06:05,411 --> 00:06:06,153
and changed,

180
00:06:06,153 --> 00:06:08,874
when the result differs from the last build.

181
00:06:08,874 --> 00:06:10,544
Start with the first three.

182
00:06:11,094 --> 00:06:12,790
Post runs after the stages,

183
00:06:12,790 --> 00:06:13,921
on the same agent,

184
00:06:13,921 --> 00:06:16,748
so it can still use the files the build made.

185
00:06:18,298 --> 00:06:20,709
Five mistakes that waste a beginner an hour.

186
00:06:21,259 --> 00:06:22,632
One: a missing brace.

187
00:06:22,632 --> 00:06:25,249
Every opening brace needs a closing one.

188
00:06:25,249 --> 00:06:27,211
Jenkins reports a line number,

189
00:06:27,211 --> 00:06:29,173
so read the first error first.

190
00:06:29,723 --> 00:06:30,528
Two: quotes.

191
00:06:30,528 --> 00:06:32,739
Use single quotes for plain text.

192
00:06:32,739 --> 00:06:35,085
Double quotes let variables expand,

193
00:06:35,085 --> 00:06:37,297
and you need that only sometimes.

194
00:06:37,847 --> 00:06:41,900
Three: a stage with no steps. Every stage needs a steps block.

195
00:06:42,450 --> 00:06:44,166
Four: passwords in the file.

196
00:06:44,166 --> 00:06:44,534
Never.

197
00:06:44,534 --> 00:06:46,556
Credentials get their own lesson,

198
00:06:46,556 --> 00:06:48,701
and the file must be safe to share.

199
00:06:49,251 --> 00:06:50,424
Five: vague names.

200
00:06:50,424 --> 00:06:54,661
Stage one and stage two tell you nothing at three in the morning.

201
00:06:54,661 --> 00:06:55,052
Build,

202
00:06:55,052 --> 00:06:56,291
Test and Deploy do.

203
00:06:57,841 --> 00:07:00,636
Where to find the lab. Pause here, and write it down.

204
00:07:01,186 --> 00:07:02,650
One: the repository,

205
00:07:02,650 --> 00:07:06,970
github.com/it-waves/devops-ai,

206
00:07:06,970 --> 00:07:09,314
in the folder academy slash lab.

207
00:07:09,864 --> 00:07:16,072
Two: the files, lab156.sh, and shortcuts156.sh.

208
00:07:16,622 --> 00:07:22,083
Three: install it, with sudo install, into /usr/local/sbin.

209
00:07:22,633 --> 00:07:24,439
Four: load the short names,

210
00:07:24,439 --> 00:07:27,381
with source shortcuts156.sh.

211
00:07:27,381 --> 00:07:31,060
Then lab files writes four ready Jenkinsfiles for you.

212
00:07:31,610 --> 00:07:35,271
Five: you need the Jenkins from Lesson 155.

213
00:07:35,271 --> 00:07:37,238
The lab files are plain text,

214
00:07:37,238 --> 00:07:39,204
to paste into a Pipeline job.

215
00:07:39,754 --> 00:07:43,552
Six: only on your own machine. Lab clean removes the files.

216
00:07:44,102 --> 00:07:45,744
The link is in the description.

217
00:07:47,794 --> 00:07:49,565
Your turn, on your own machine.

218
00:07:50,115 --> 00:07:53,784
One: start Jenkins from Lesson 155, and sign in.

219
00:07:54,334 --> 00:07:57,918
Two: create a Pipeline job, type version one, and build it.

220
00:07:58,468 --> 00:08:00,921
Three: grow it to Build, Test and Deploy.

221
00:08:01,471 --> 00:08:04,351
Four: add post, with always, success and failure.

222
00:08:04,901 --> 00:08:08,421
Five: make Test fail on purpose, and find the red stage.

223
00:08:08,971 --> 00:08:10,806
Pause the video, and begin now.

224
00:08:26,356 --> 00:08:27,231
Quick check.

225
00:08:27,781 --> 00:08:30,021
Name the five parts of a Jenkinsfile.

226
00:08:30,571 --> 00:08:33,344
What is the difference between declarative and scripted?

227
00:08:33,894 --> 00:08:36,881
What happens to later stages when one stage fails?

228
00:08:37,431 --> 00:08:40,097
Which post condition runs only on a failure?

229
00:08:40,647 --> 00:08:43,160
Objective covered: MC17,

230
00:08:43,160 --> 00:08:44,378
objective three.

231
00:08:44,378 --> 00:08:46,738
Writing a Jenkinsfile: stages,

232
00:08:46,738 --> 00:08:47,195
steps,

233
00:08:47,195 --> 00:08:48,413
agents and post.

234
00:08:48,963 --> 00:08:51,032
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

235
00:09:01,582 --> 00:09:05,870
Next: building the CloudCart application with Maven, in Jenkins.

236
00:09:06,420 --> 00:09:09,748
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
